  <data name="Lhdc_HelpDownload.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>1. Pair your earbuds in Windows, then select Download LHDC component. Downloading does not install drivers. Once the download finishes, choose the audio device above. This selection is separate from the Home page; only one device can be managed at a time.</value></data>
  <data name="Lhdc_HelpQuality.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>3. Play some audio and check that it sounds right before changing the settings. If audio stutters, lower the bitrate first. Adaptive bitrate adjusts to the connection, using your selected value as the upper limit. After each change, select Enable LHDC / apply settings again. Select Refresh to check the current status; the earbuds’ Hi-Res switch does not confirm that Windows is using LHDC.</value></data>
  <data name="Lhdc_HelpApply.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>2. Try 48 kHz, 24 bit and 400 kbps first. Select Enable LHDC / apply settings and approve the Windows administrator prompt. Installation checks whether your earbuds support LHDC V5. Sound will briefly stop during the switch; wait for it to finish. If asked to restart, restart your PC, then select Refresh.</value></data>
  <data name="Lhdc_HelpRequirements.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>You need the non-Store x64 edition of EncoDeck, Windows 11 22H2 (22621) or later, and earbuds that support LHDC V5. Windows may block the test-signed drivers from loading. EncoDeck does not automatically change test mode, Secure Boot or memory integrity settings.</value></data>
  <data name="Lhdc_Help.Header" xml:space="preserve"><value>How to use LHDC</value></data>
  <data name="Lhdc_HelpRestore.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>4. Before you stop using LHDC or uninstall EncoDeck, select the earbuds, choose Restore Windows audio, approve administrator access and wait for it to finish. Closing EncoDeck does not stop the LHDC background service or restore Windows audio.</value></data>
  <data name="Lhdc_Disclaimer.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Disclaimer: LHDC is an experimental feature based on the third-party open-source project LHDC-Win. Playback, reconnection and recovery still need hardware testing; compatibility with every device or system is not guaranteed. You may encounter lost audio, stuttering or a need to restore audio manually. Use it only if you understand and accept the risks of driver installation and system setting changes. To the extent permitted by applicable law, EncoDeck and its developers are not liable for resulting losses.</value></data>
