  <data name="Bridge_Adapters.Header" xml:space="preserve"><value>Adapters</value></data>
  <data name="Bridge_AdaptersNote.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>A2DPWB uses only a dedicated USB adapter with the WinUSB driver; Windows no longer uses it once assigned. EncoDeck never assigns the adapter Windows' Bluetooth is using. Realtek adapters also need firmware files: put them in the settings folder and click Refresh.</value></data>
  <data name="Bridge_FirmwareLink.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>Realtek firmware downloads</value></data>
  <data name="Bridge_Disclaimer.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Disclaimer: A2DP Windows Bridge is a third-party open-source project by Seiya Funaoka (Apache-2.0 / MIT); this component is an unmodified copy of its official build. The BTstack library it links statically is licensed for personal, non-commercial use only. Earbud compatibility, radio quality and adapter firmware are outside EncoDeck's control; use it at your own risk.</value></data>
  <data name="Bridge_Help.Header" xml:space="preserve"><value>How to use A2DP Bridge</value></data>
  <data name="Bridge_HelpRequirements.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>You need a dedicated USB Bluetooth adapter, separate from the PC's built-in Bluetooth (upstream verified the TP-Link UB500 / Realtek RTL8761BU), and Windows 10 22H2 or Windows 11. Never assign WinUSB to the built-in Bluetooth.</value></data>
  <data name="Bridge_HelpSetup.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>1. After downloading and starting the background process, assign WinUSB to the dedicated adapter under Adapters (administrator rights); Realtek adapters also need their firmware files. Then click "Open A2DPWB" to add your earbuds, choose a codec and connect in its window.</value></data>
  <data name="Bridge_HelpDaily.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>2. From then on, connect and disconnect the earbuds, switch profiles and change the volume in the A2DPWB window. By default A2DPWB sends what Windows plays; closing EncoDeck does not stop the audio. You can let EncoDeck start the A2DPWB background process.</value></data>
  <data name="Bridge_HelpControls.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>3. Over the dedicated adapter Windows cannot see the earbuds. A2DPWB 2.0.2 and later opens the control channel for EncoDeck: the home page shows battery and earbud settings as usual, with an A2DP Bridge badge, and earbuds paired only in A2DPWB appear in EncoDeck too. Earbuds with dual-device connection can also connect to the built-in Bluetooth; then set Windows' default output to another device so that two streams do not reach the earbuds.</value></data>
  <data name="Bridge_HelpRestore.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>4. When you stop using it, click "Restore driver" under Adapters to give the adapter back to Windows, then quit the background process. Uninstalling EncoDeck does not restore the driver.</value></data>
