  <data name="Lhdc_UninstallBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>This deletes the LHDC component files EncoDeck downloaded (about 120 MB). Enabling LHDC or restoring Windows audio needs them, so they can only be removed once every headset is back on Windows audio. The disabled LHDC service and driver packages in Windows are left as they are.</value></data>
  <data name="Lhdc_UninstallInUse" xml:space="preserve"><value>LHDC is still enabled: choose the headset that uses it and click Restore Windows audio first, then uninstall the component.</value></data>
  <data name="Lhdc_UninstallBound" xml:space="preserve"><value>These audio devices still use the LHDC driver: {0}. Restore Windows audio first, then uninstall the component.</value></data>
